x was referring to  um it's very bright and white and  reflective so  the ice caps in general and polar ice in  the polar regions reflects lots of  incoming sunlight so when we start to  lose  that sea ice um it exposes the dark  ocean water beneath it and that absorbs  much more  energy and radiation uh which starts to  melt the sea ice even faster so it's  this  mutu this reinforcing positive feedback  where  warming causes loss of sea ice which  causes more warming which causes more  loss of sea ice  and this has quite far-reaching impacts  beyond just the arctic  it influences the weather in the  northern hemisphere for example and it  influences things like  the ocean circulation so it's not just a  case of what happens in the arctic stays  in the arctic it's  an effect and a phenomenon with really  global consequences  yeah the ripples go far and wide don't  they dr ella gilbert good to talk to you  thank you  well in the next four years all  electricity in britain could be 100  powered by renewables for a period of  time that's according  to the national grid electricity system  operator report  well let's take a look now at our data  dashboard it shows us the real-time  power mix  in great britain at the moment you can  see that 32  of electricity is coming from fossil  fuels 49  of it from renewable sources and 19  from nuclear but the study shows that at  times that number there that fossil  fuels number could be zero  in britain in 2025 meaning renewables  could account for 100 of electricity  during a period of time let's find out  how  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  uh  [Music]  and helping to hit those targets and  indeed happening  this very week the world's longest  undersea  electric cable is being switched on this  week with testing already underway  the 720 kilometer interconnector  will be used to trade clean energy  between the uk and norway  as part of plans to cut emissions and  boost offshore wind  interconnectors supplied around 8 of uk  power i