ts we are  encouraging governments to  step up and take more action and  scale up the ambition nato before we  talk a lot don't we about g7  countries and what they're doing in  their move towards renewables  it sounds like we very much should be  talking about countries like south  africa as well  yes countries like south africa are  really um joining  the big countries in terms of uh the the  shift in the way that we generate power  and we are shifting from the coal-fired  generation to clean energy we've got  very ambitious targets  as a country uh which also follows a  global trend because we see it globally  that there is a shift  from um a coal-fired power to  to to a sources like wind energy  and solar energy and therefore south  africa  is obviously benefiting from the global  action but there is a lot happening on  the ground  in the country we've seen a lot in terms  of shift  in the policy space we have seen a lot  of action happening and a government  really taking action  to to to to scale up their ambition and  to  approve policies that were actually  blocking the deployment of renewable  energy  natomi flutie naturally really good to  talk to you thank you  well let's take a look at some of the  day's other climate news now  and the government's being warned that  its free trade deal with australia could  have a devastating impact on the  environment  green campaigners have raised concerns  the agreement could lead to increased  carbon emissions  and undermine britain's animal welfare  standards they say the government should  not reward countries without dating  farming systems which are fueling  the climate crisis wildfires in the  rocky mountains  are burning more frequently now than in  the past  2000 years that's according to a new  study by the university of montana  and wyoming scientists there collected  data from high elevation forests in  colorado  and compared it with fire history  records they believe  wildfire activity has increased due t